WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:21.080
This technology has been used
in a wide variety of fields.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.710
I know it has an application
in manufacturing,

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.190
and but the way that we,
as a club, borderline

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.790
uses AR is basically
to kind of reveal

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:34.750
second meaning or hidden layer
behind the student murals.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.950
The way that it works is you
see this animation playing

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.560
on your phone through
the app that we use.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:47.330
It can reveal something that
previously wasn't there.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:51.270
One day I just
realized that this,

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:55.440
because of this kind of desire
to know these microorganisms

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.280
better, I use the term empathy.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.350
How do I empathize
with the bacteria

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:05.220
that is constituting me in a
very material and literal way.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.680
And then I suddenly
realized that I would never

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.410
reach that state of empathy
with my bacteria in a Petri dish

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:17.010
because then it's a specimen,
and it's specifying itself

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.160
as a specimen.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.030
And that power dynamic
and that relationship,

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.090
that would never
constitute something

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.640
close to empathy, or
wonder, or surprise,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.430
because the object
is already known.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:33.383
There's a way that data is kind
of treated as found material.

00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:34.550
It's just kind of out there.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:39.850
But you know, it's not exactly
the same as just picking

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.320
something up off the street.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.710
It's something that's
usually highly integrated

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:45.680
into people's lives.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.300
And so there's these like
questions of consent and power

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:50.140
that are baked in.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:53.480
And I think that's where
they converge for me.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.960
Being an artist and
being creative at MIT,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.750
also like understanding
the ethics and implications

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:03.140
of the technologies that we
use, the technologies we create,

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.780
and the technologies
that are on our service,

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.280
let's say, for creation.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:11.600
So for example, got like this
like microgravity flights.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.610
They consume a lot of like
immense amounts of fossil fuel,

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:17.780
for example.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.530
There's a lot of CO2 emissions
out of these experiments.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.200
And then it makes us wonder
and putting a balance

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.500
what, what does it mean, right.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:28.632
And what does it mean?

00:02:28.632 --> 00:02:30.590
Is the work that we're
doing, are the questions

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:32.960
that we're asking
is relevant enough

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.840
to also fit in that balance and
make it in these perspectives

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.490
that we need to be
wondering about,

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.150
that we need to
be creating with.

